findings.fix:
Bound the title and fix the rag, but do not use `balance` or `anywhere`.

.cardTitle — add:
  text-wrap: pretty;
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
  display: -webkit-box;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  overflow: hidden;
Keep `flex: 1 1 auto`.

.cardNote (line 350) — add `text-wrap: pretty;` alongside the clamp it already has.

Why pretty, not balance: `pretty` prevents the single-word last line while leaving earlier lines filled to the measure, so it composes with the 2-line clamp instead of fighting it. `balance` would shorten every line before the clamp cuts, leaving the LONG TITLE specimen as two under-filled lines with an ellipsis and an open right gutter.

Why break-word, not anywhere: these are English task titles, not URLs. `break-word` catches a pathological unbreakable string without licensing mid-word breaks in ordinary copy, and it does not alter min-content sizing inside the flex row.

One interaction to resolve while doing it, because the clamp exposes it: "Chase the florist quote for the spring date" is only 43 characters yet already runs three lines, because `.when` (flex: none) crowds line 1. Under a hard 2-line clamp that title would truncate — a short title losing words is a worse failure than the widow it replaces. Raise `.cardTitle { min-width }` from 62% until that specimen fits two lines with its chip present, and re-shoot locked-dense to confirm no card in any tray truncates below ~50 characters. Also add the clamp to `[data-density="compact"] .cardTitle` (line 638) so compact bounds the title as well as the note.

Then the caption at line 971 — "Two lines of title, then the note clamps. A card cannot run away" — becomes true rather than aspirational.

Reserve two lines on the empty paragraph, accounting for border-box and expressed in the element's own units so it survives a reworded message or a type-scale change:

.trayEmpty {
  padding: 8px 4px 4px;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  line-height: 1.45;
  /* two lines, always: the Add task row must not move with sentence length */
  min-height: calc(1.45em * 2 + 12px);   /* 2 line boxes + the 8px/4px padding = 48.25px */
}

1.45em resolves against .trayEmpty's own 12.5px, so it is exactly one line box; the +12px is the vertical padding the border box now has to carry. One-line and two-line trays both land at 48.25px and all five Add task rows share one baseline. Verify by re-shooting locked-empty--1440x960.png and confirming the five .trayAdd rows report an identical getBoundingClientRect().top. Harmless in the other states (trayEmpty only renders for an empty column) and on <=720px, where trays scroll one at a time.

Kill the two sources of truth rather than patching the second one. In floor.html add a single task-set accessor and derive every header fact from it:

  function allTasks() {
    if (state === "empty") return [];
    return state === "dense" ? B.tasks.concat(window.DENSE_EXTRA) : B.tasks.slice();
  }
  function tasksFor(lane) {
    return allTasks().map((t, i) => (t.id ? t : { ...t, id: "x" + i })).filter((t) => t.lane === lane);
  }

Then in head():

  const rows    = allTasks();
  const total   = rows.length;
  const done    = rows.filter((t) => t.lane === "done").length;
  const overdue = rows.filter((t) => t.dueTone === "overdue").length;

and render `overdue` into the .late chip in place of `p.overdue`, keeping the chip suppressed when overdue === 0 (today it is suppressed only for the empty state; a real workspace with nothing late should not show a "0 overdue" ink chip). `p.day`, `p.of` and `p.undated` stay fixture values — they are calendar facts, not counts of the rendered set.

In timeOf, replace the literal with the task's own data, guarded so a missing `due` cannot render " late" alone:

  if (task.dueTone === "overdue") {
    const late = String(task.due || "").replace(/^Overdue by\s*/i, "").trim();
    return { kind: "overdue", label: late ? late + " late" : "Late" };
  }

Apply the same change to direction-a.html, direction-b.html, direction-c.html and customizer.html — all four carry the identical `const done = state === "empty" ? 0 : p.done;` line, and leaving the customizer unfixed would have it contradict the locked board it is meant to explain. Re-shoot locked-dense--1440x960.png afterwards; the header must read "9 of 32 done" and match the DONE tray count exactly.

Two changes, both inside the ≤720px block.

1. Make the header rows explicit AND make the top row actually fit — the grid alone does not.

Replace lines 717-722 with:

  .head {
    height: auto; display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    grid-template-areas: "word actions" "name name" "facts facts";
    align-items: center; column-gap: 12px; row-gap: 10px;
    padding: 14px 16px 0;
  }
  .word { grid-area: word; }
  .headActions { grid-area: actions; justify-self: end; margin-left: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .headName { grid-area: name; width: auto; font-size: 19px; }
  .headFacts { grid-area: facts; display: flex; font-size: 12.5px; gap: 12px; }
  .headRule { display: none; }
  .headSearch { display: inline-flex; }

Delete every `order:` declaration in the block. Note `auto 1fr` (not `1fr auto`): the wordmark is fixed at its own width and the flexible column absorbs slack, so the row never depends on the action cluster's intrinsic width.

Then shed the ~40px that caused the wrap in the first place, so the top row has real slack at 375w:

  .headActions .ghost span { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); }
  .headActions .ghost { padding: 0 9px; }

i.e. on phone, Share / Planning / more become icon-only (the count `em` on Planning stays visible — it is the only number in that cluster and carries the signal). Labels remain in the accessible name via the existing text, so keep `aria-label` on Share and Planning to match. This drops the cluster from ~280px to ~150px and the header top row fits comfortably from 320w up. Verify at 320, 360, 375, 390 and 430 before calling it done.

2. Fade the view strip so it reads as scrollable, and hide the scrollbar:

  .views {
    padding: 14px 16px 12px; overflow-x: auto;
    scrollbar-width: none;
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 calc(100% - 28px), transparent);
            mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #000 calc(100% - 28px), transparent);
  }
  .views::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

The mask is alpha only, so it introduces no hue and does not touch the palette lock. Prefer masking on both edges driven by scroll position if you want it exact, but a right-only fade is correct here because four tabs never fit at ≤720 and the strip therefore always overflows to the right at rest.

Net effect: three bands instead of four, ~40px returned to the board, no orphaned wordmark line, no action cluster floating against nothing, and the clipped tab reads as content continuing rather than as a rendering bug.

Inset the sheet and let the board scroll rather than crush — reuse the rule that already exists at the 1200px breakpoint, and add no new shadow token.

.floor:has(.drawer) .sheet {
  margin-right: calc(388px + var(--inset));
  transition: margin-right var(--dur) var(--curve);
}
.floor:has(.drawer) .board {
  grid-template-columns: none;
  grid-auto-flow: column;
  grid-auto-columns: minmax(258px, 1fr);
}
@media (max-width: 1200px) {
  .floor:has(.drawer) .sheet { margin-right: calc(364px + var(--inset)); }
}
@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .floor:has(.drawer) .sheet { margin-right: 0; }
}

This draws the seam with the product's own declared elevation logic — the ink floor shows through an 18px gutter between sheet and drawer, which is precisely the rule stated at line 187: "White against ink needs no shadow. Contrast is the elevation." So DROP the proposed `0 8px 40px rgba(17,17,17,0.10)`: it is a single soft blur (forbidden at line 96) at an alpha off the stated ladder, and once the sheet is inset it is not needed at all.

Insetting the sheet also fixes three things the finding lists separately and one it misses, for free: the DONE column and its 5 cards return, the WAITING note is no longer clipped, the dock (left:50% of the sheet) re-centres on the board it belongs to, and the Planning trigger in headActions becomes visible again — so give it `aria-expanded="true"` and `aria-controls` pointing at the drawer while it is open.

Below 900px, where `.drawer { left: var(--inset); width: auto }` already makes it a full-width overlay, it does need declared elevation and a scrim — but use the tokens that exist: `box-shadow: var(--ring), var(--float);` plus an ink scrim at an alpha already on the ladder, not an invented blur.

Markup: `<aside class="drawer" aria-label="Planning">` (floor.html:918) — as written in the finding, correct.
